Z||000008||Capt Fiero||09-26-2001||02:47 PM||||Man this topic comes up each year or so, I am going to make it as brief as possible.  <P>1st removing your EGR system DOES help in throttle response, noticbly in stick shift cars.<P>2nd It DOES increas your emmisions, NOX is most apparent.<P>3rd It CAN cause damage, I am not going to say it WILL but it can.  Your EGR sends a small amount of "HOT" ehaust gas back into your intake, that exhaust gas displaces some of the Oxygen/Air as it goes into your combustion chamber, having less usable oxygen in your combustion chamber makes your fuel - air charge burn cooler.  So by removing that exhaust gas, you are now increasing the amount of "Good" air/oxy in your combustion chamber and that makes your combustion Hotter.  That extra heat is what CAN cause damage.  Idle there will be noticable diffrece, at Wide open throttle it wont hurt anyrthing either.  But at sustained medium load, part throttle operations (IE going up a hill in 3rd gear at 4000rpms using only part throttle, it can cause your to literaly melt a piston.  It is almost the same thing as running a NOS kit with too small of a fuel jet. (just not nearly as severe) I ran my 85GT without an EGR for 3 years then tore the motor down for a bad rod bearing and there was sighns of extremly high combustion chamber temps, (piston was heat scored and partially melted around the edges) If your car has more than a few miles on it, and has at least some cylnder blow-bye, your motor is getting enough bad gasses that your EGR removal will be more than lickly safe, heck you may even pass your local emmsions test. (I did) just make sure that you leave the componants in tact, if they see they are gone, most lickly they will fail you on the spot. <P>Last but not least.  If you want to block the EGR system on a V6.  Easy was is remove the EGR pipe at the EGR valve, place a small piece of tin, between the pipe mating surface and the EGR pod then re-assemble. I used a  (cut up pop can) wrapped in foil (renyolds wrap aluminum foil works really well)  When you are done you should have a quarter silver doller sized round EGR stopper.  If you want to be more creative, you can pull your plenium off and block both ends of the pipe so you can be sure that you wont get a vaccume leak if the EGR pipe ever cracks, and you will never get an exhaust leak from the other end as the computer will still be opening and closing the valve.<P>------------------<BR>David<BR>85GT<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://photos.onebigvillage.com/pubimage.asp?id_=32415"> <p>[This message has been edited by Capt Fiero (edited 09-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fallman||09-26-2001||07:39 PM||||I pulled the hole thing out including pipe and valve capped at the crossover pipe and the underside of the upper intake.<P>As to the to much heat. <P>I just rebuilt this engine.  so HELP i dont want to be pulling it apart in a few days would this be why it was overheating really badly right after rebuild. and not so bad after i removed thermostat. but when it idles it still over heats i think it was partly due to a plugged raditor.<P>but thats fixed i have to put tranny in and then i will see if its still overheating but i dont want to kill this engine||||reg||1
Z||000010||87GTZ34||09-26-2001||09:56 PM||||The ECM is setup to include the EGR for both control and for A/F.  While closed loop will compensate, under slight load you will still get pinging unless you have an after market prom to compensate even more.  You also could run 92 octane if want to put a couple of extra bucks in the gas tank when refueling (not me these days). I considered bypassing mine but after evaluating the circumstances for it, ended up repairing it.  I don't think blocking it off gives ANY extra horses at all as it doesn't open at WOT anyway.  It just keeps emissions down under slight load by not having to add extra fuel to due the same.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Fiero_Freak||09-27-2001||09:19 PM||||If it's overheating around town that's one thing. I know my egr caused mine to heat up slowly and then overheat if I was going down the highway.  I know it's wierd but the hot gases accually make the thing run cooler!  Go figure.  I didn't notice much of a lag after I got it working, but I did get 5 MPH better gas mileage.<P>||||reg||1
